or them  so when we chose these schools and we  did daily wastewater monitoring in these  schools  we also did surface swaps where we  swapped high touch surfaces  like doorknobs like desks and these high  test surfaces  in these schools and the other thing was  we chose these schools also  based on the occupancy of these schools  these schools were operating at near  full capacity  during the pandemic and the positivity  rates in these zip codes were upwards of  25  so we also did weekly diagnostic testing  for all of the staff the teachers as  well as the students  in the program and we did diagnostic  testing because we wanted to validate  our  environmental testing with diagnostic  testing to see  if we missed any samples that were not  picked up  so on the right you see one of our auto  samplers in one of these elementary  schools which the  students put stickers and drew and  personalized  and on the left you see a map of all the  schools that are currently enrolled in  our pilot program  and as you can see they're very  diverse and they're split all over the  san diego county  just slightly more focused near the  water because the  prevalence rates there were higher but  their testing rates were lower  and currently we're using this data to  see if you can prevent outbreaks on  schools and so far none of these schools  in spite of operating at full capacity  have had  significant outbreaks because of the  constant  monitoring and surveillance and  this brings me to the last portion of  this talk and also it shows us which  direction we're heading currently  so while using rtq pcr is a good uh  method to track source code e2 cases  um viral genome sequencing will help us  to actually see which are the strains in  circulation are there any variants of  concern  are there multiple people contributing  to the waste stream or is it just one  person  so in order to do that we'll have to  look at the viral genome sequence from  wastewater  and it's much easier to look at it from  wastewater because  sam like um sequencing one wastewater  sample is  essentially getting the sequences of  every single person in that building who  might be infected compared to a nasal  swab where one person is  equal to just one strain so what we're  doing now is  we are doing moral genome sequencing  using  a tiled amplicon approach to get the  whole genome from wastewater  and in the right you will see snapshots  of some of her  phylogeny so the one i've highlighted in  green  shows the wastewater samples viral  genome sequence  and you can see it's identical to  another sequence and that other sequence  was actually sequenced  from the nasal swab of a person who  tested positive  and was in the same building whose  wastewater we tested  so this gives us more confidence in  using wastewater  as a monitoring tool for tracking  variants  especially when you have a lot of  variance of concerns circulating  so based on our analysis so far we've  processed about 400  samples and we're going to be processing  about 2 000 more  over the next month and from our point  loma point loma is our san diego  county's wastewater treatment plant  from the wastewater that we survey every  day these are all the lineages we  detected  we do see a few variants of concern and  these are samples that they're dated up  till april  so we're still processing the may and  june samples now  the sassy is the school elementary  school program and these are all the  variants that we're seeing from  wastewater  wastewater samples from the same schools  as you can see  there are a couple of variants of  concern and we saw b117  um or the alpha variant or the uk  variant  much ahead of when we saw it in the  diagnostic samples actually  so with that um i want to conclude the  talk  um but i also want to thank all the  people who made  this um whole program happen it's a  multidisciplinary effort  and without all of these people and  their help you wouldn't have been able  to  se